WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.167
Gabrielle SIERRA: Does the U.S.&nbsp;
consider Taiwan a country?
&nbsp;

00:00:06.167 --> 00:00:08.700
David SACKS: No. 
 
SIERRA: Okay. But it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.840
possible that we may go to war&nbsp;
with our most powerful adversary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:18.420
to defend its independence. 
SACKS: To defend the status quo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:23.283
and its de facto independence. Yes. 
CBN News: Let’s begin this half hour in Asia where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.283 --> 00:00:27.780
Taiwan is getting ready for war with China.
CBN News: A popular saying among people here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:32.700
is Ukraine today, Taiwan tomorrow.
ABC News: President Biden, last fall, saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:38.400
for the first time that if China invades Taiwan&nbsp;
the U.S. would send troops to defend it.
&nbsp;

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:49.560
Xi Jinping: [Speaking in Mandarin]
SIERRA:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:59.460
Tensions between China and Taiwan are one of the&nbsp;
world’s major fault lines. And in recent years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:04.020
those tensions have been ratcheting up. 
SIERRA: China feels that Taiwan is part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:08.760
its territory, and has vowed to one day&nbsp;
reclaim it, and the history underneath that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:15.840
claim is complex and tense. To make matters more&nbsp;
confusing, the U.S. stance on Taiwan has been kept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:21.840
purposefully vague for decades - a policy that&nbsp;
has come to be known as strategic ambiguity. 
&nbsp;

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:27.660
SIERRA: Mixed up in all of this are the Taiwanese&nbsp;
people themselves, who of course have their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:32.940
nuanced perspectives on the situation. 
SIERRA: My name is Gabrielle Sierra and this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:39.720
is Why It Matters. Today a crash course on Taiwan,&nbsp;
and a glimpse into what a Chinese invasion could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:41.740
mean for the world.
SACKS:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:56.040
A Chinese invasion of Taiwan would likely be the&nbsp;
most complex modern military operation, something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:01.920
that makes D-Day look small by comparison. 
SIERRA: This is David Sacks - you actually heard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:07.740
him at the top of the episode. He’s a fellow here&nbsp;
at the Council focusing on Taiwan and China. He’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:13.740
the director of the Council’s new Independent&nbsp;
Task Force report on Taiwan, which formed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:17.760
basis for this episode. You should totally&nbsp;
go check it out in the show notes page. 
&nbsp;

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:23.460
SACKS: We're talking about tens of thousands,&nbsp;
if not hundreds of thousands of Chinese soldiers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:30.360
transiting the Taiwan Strait, Taiwan responding&nbsp;
by using force of its own. And we're likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:36.120
talking about large-scale casualties on both&nbsp;
sides. If the United States were to intervene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:41.520
on Taiwan's behalf, I think there should be&nbsp;
no doubt that we're talking about casualties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:46.920
on the U.S. side that make Iraq and Afghanistan&nbsp;
look small by comparison. And there's of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:53.520
the large unknown, which is what happens when two&nbsp;
nuclear armed powers face off directly against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:58.800
one another. Something that hasn't occurred&nbsp;
yet in history. China's military has advanced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:05.220
capabilities from anti-ship ballistic missiles&nbsp;
to hypersonics and advanced fighter jets, all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:11.280
which would make U.S. defense of Taiwan incredibly&nbsp;
difficult. When people think about this conflict,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:17.040
people often say that there's the potential that&nbsp;
you would see U.S. aircraft carriers at the bottom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:22.440
of the ocean with thousands of casualties&nbsp;
as a result just of that alone. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:27.720
it's hard to know for sure whether China could&nbsp;
put its capabilities to use and make that happen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:34.080
but we know for a fact that China is attempting&nbsp;
to do just that - hold major U.S. assets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:42.000
including aircraft carriers at risk.
SIERRA: War between the United States and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:46.920
China. It’s not inevitable, but it is&nbsp;
possible. And even without military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:52.380
conflict between the U.S. and China, an&nbsp;
invasion of Taiwan has the power to stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:58.020
the global economy in its tracks. 
SACKS: A Chinese blockade or invasion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:03.360
the moment it starts, the world's busiest&nbsp;
shipping lanes grind to a halt. We would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:09.480
talking about a global economic depression that&nbsp;
would not only shave trillions of dollars off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:15.000
global GDP in the first year, but I would argue&nbsp;
it would be incredibly difficult to recover and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:20.100
reverse the economic impact. The other thing that&nbsp;
I would say is the impact that this would have on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:26.520
international order. We've seen one authoritarian&nbsp;
country attack a democratic neighbor and attempt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:32.880
to grab territory. If China did the same to&nbsp;
Taiwan, this would set a pattern of authoritarian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:38.760
countries doing so. We look at Taiwan as a vibrant&nbsp;
democracy in a region of the world where there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:45.480
aren't many democracies. By some measures, Taiwan&nbsp;
is the freest society in Asia, and a Chinese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:50.820
assault on Taiwan and annexation of Taiwan would&nbsp;
extinguish a democracy of twenty-three million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:55.860
And I think that would have pretty chilling&nbsp;
effects for other young democracies around the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.860 --> 00:05:00.840
world. Of course, there would be massive human&nbsp;
costs for the twenty-three million people of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:06.540
Taiwan as well that we should keep in mind. And&nbsp;
so I think some might say, "Well, this is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:12.360
my problem," but I think that this is all of our&nbsp;
problems. You should have no doubt that this would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:21.180
have profound implications for your life. 
SIERRA: I think that a lot of Americans have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:27.540
of course, heard of Taiwan but don't necessarily&nbsp;
know much beyond the headlines. So I was wondering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:32.820
if you could humor me with some basics.
James LIN: Sure. So Taiwan is an island off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:38.160
the southeastern coast of China, about&nbsp;
a hundred miles away. It's not a small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:41.580
island. It's not a large island. I believe it's&nbsp;
around the size of the state of Maryland.
&nbsp;

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:47.040
SIERRA: This is James Lin. He’s an assistant&nbsp;
professor of international studies and history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:51.180
at the University of Washington where&nbsp;
he teaches and researches Taiwan.
&nbsp;

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:59.520
LIN: So Taiwan is in a multi-party democracy. Many&nbsp;
of the kinds of democratic indexes like V-Dem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:03.480
which is one that many political scientists&nbsp;
rely on, rates Taiwan as a highly free,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:08.220
highly democratic society. 
SIERRA: Is Taiwan its own country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:13.380
or is it part of China?
SACKS: Well, that depends on who you ask. Only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:18.540
around a dozen countries in the world recognize&nbsp;
Taiwan as a sovereign independent country and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:23.520
United States is not one of those countries.&nbsp;
The United States does not recognize it as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:29.460
sovereign independent country. But if you walk&nbsp;
down the streets of Taipei or anywhere in Taiwan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:35.280
it looks and acts like a country. It has its own&nbsp;
military, its own currency and elected government.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:41.640
And of course, to the Taiwanese people who live on&nbsp;
the island, Taiwan is very much its own country.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:47.820
To the People's Republic of China, Taiwan is&nbsp;
essentially a renegade province, something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:56.460
must be restored to the PRC by force if necessary.&nbsp;
So for the United States, our position is a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.460 --> 00:07:04.620
difficult to understand, but it is that Taiwan's&nbsp;
status is undetermined. So we haven't reached the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:10.020
endpoint yet at which point you could determine&nbsp;
whether Taiwan is a part of China, an independent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:13.740
entity, or something else. 
SIERRA: The stakes are high,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:19.920
and China’s sensitivity to the issue is so&nbsp;
strong, that diplomats the world over walk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:24.240
on eggshells when discussing Taiwan. 
SIERRA: In fact, we don’t usually do this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:28.740
but check out this moment from a recent&nbsp;
CFR event in which Secretary of State&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:34.860
Antony Blinken tactfully reinforces the&nbsp;
United States’ ambiguous stance to our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:40.440
president emeritus Richard Haass: 
Richard HAASS: So four times your boss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:45.240
the President of the United States, has said&nbsp;
that the U.S. would help defend Taiwan if it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:50.880
were attacked by China. And four times the White&nbsp;
House staff has walked him back. So what is our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:56.700
policy? And Why aren’t we basically telling China,&nbsp;
within the context of a One China policy, you’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.700 --> 00:08:02.160
done things to increase the threat, we’re going to&nbsp;
do things to increase your understanding that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:05.400
cannot move against Taiwan with impunity?
Antony BLINKEN: Richard...
&nbsp;

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:09.900
HAASS: Yes, sir?
BLINKEN: We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:14.220
continue to be guided by the One China&nbsp;
policy based on the Three Communiques,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:17.700
the Taiwan Relations Act, and the Six Assurances.&nbsp;
That hasn’t changed and that won’t change.
&nbsp;

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:25.920
SACKS: The Biden administration has been quite&nbsp;
consistent that it still maintains the U.S. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:31.500
China policy. But at the same time, there have&nbsp;
been questions about whether strategic ambiguity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:36.360
remains U.S. policy, which is different than&nbsp;
the One China policy. President Biden on four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:41.760
occasions has seemed to endorse what we would&nbsp;
call strategic clarity, saying that if China&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:47.940
attacked Taiwan, he would respond and defend&nbsp;
Taiwan. Even though the administration walked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:54.060
it back on each occasion, to me, it's important&nbsp;
that we take note of that. So my own view is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:59.940
we shouldn't view President Biden's statements&nbsp;
as gaffes. I think that he fully understands the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:05.280
implications of what he says. This is a man who&nbsp;
has an experienced foreign policy track record,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:11.100
was on the Senate Foreign Relations Committee. In&nbsp;
fact, when George W. Bush said a similar thing as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:16.380
president, then Senator Joe Biden wrote an op-ed&nbsp;
in the Washington Post criticizing him for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:21.180
lack of discipline on this question. So in my&nbsp;
view, President Biden has been very clear and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.180 --> 00:09:27.780
has said on four times that if he were faced with&nbsp;
this monumental decision as commander-in-chief of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:33.060
whether to come to Taiwan's defense, he would&nbsp;
do so. And I think that it's important that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:37.080
take him at his word, and I think that the Chinese&nbsp;
also take him at his word, and they believe that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:41.160
he was serious with what he said.
SIERRA: How have other countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:45.060
treated this situation? 
LIN: So today, most countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:50.100
deal with Taiwan on an unofficial basis. There&nbsp;
are a small number of nations - a little bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:55.080
over a dozen - who still continue to maintain&nbsp;
diplomatic relations with Taiwan only. Usually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.080 --> 00:10:02.100
these are small South Pacific or Latin American&nbsp;
nations. And Beijing has sometimes tried to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:07.200
convince them to switch recognition in different&nbsp;
ways. So very recently, there's been some changes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:11.760
around that. But for the most part, Taiwan is not&nbsp;
recognized as a country, and so has to conduct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:18.120
relations in this kind of informal fashion.
SIERRA: Okay, so it’s not just the U.S. Most of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:22.380
the world is in the same touchy dilemma.&nbsp;
They can’t afford to acknowledge Taiwan as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:28.140
a country openly - the cost would be far too&nbsp;
high in terms of China’s reaction. And yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.140 --> 00:10:33.420
at the same time, many countries engage in&nbsp;
warm, if careful, relations with Taiwan. 
&nbsp;

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:39.240
SIERRA: To understand this strange balance,&nbsp;
and the potential for war if it fails, we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:44.520
to turn to history. How did we get here? 
SIERRA: First thing’s first, indigenous people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:50.220
have resided in Taiwan for thousands of years. And&nbsp;
they are still an important part of the island’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:56.580
culture. But, through the centuries, Taiwan was&nbsp;
repeatedly colonized, by the Chinese, the Dutch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.580 --> 00:11:02.460
and most recently by Japan in 1895. But the&nbsp;
trends that explain the current situation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:08.820
really begin after World War II, when China was&nbsp;
consumed by a bloody civil war that ultimately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:13.080
led to the ascension of the Chinese Communist&nbsp;
Party that governs the country today.
 
&nbsp;

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:18.360
Real Time History: The Qing Dynasty had ruled&nbsp;
China for nearly four hundred years, but the old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:23.640
ways were coming to an end. Republicanism,&nbsp;
war-lordism, nationalism, and communism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:29.880
struggled for supremacy through revolution and&nbsp;
a twenty-two year civil war between an ambitious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:36.300
generalissimo and a guerilla revolutionary.
SACKS: After World War II, the Chinese Civil War&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.300 --> 00:11:42.660
really takes shape, pitting Mao's Communists&nbsp;
against Chiang Kai-shek and the Nationalists.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:47.340
The United States sides with Chiang Kai-shek and&nbsp;
the Nationalists because they were allies during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.340 --> 00:11:52.800
World War II. But for a number of reasons, the&nbsp;
Nationalists lose out to the Communists in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:59.520
Civil War, and Chiang Kai-shek and the KMT&nbsp;
and his followers retreat to Taiwan in 1949,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:03.900
as Mao declares the establishment of the&nbsp;
People's Republic of China that same year.
&nbsp;

00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:13.260
Mao Zedong: [Speaking in Mandarin]
SACKS: Now, at that point, the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.260 --> 00:12:17.580
States was frustrated with Chiang Kai-shek,&nbsp;
a sense that he didn't take our advice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.580 --> 00:12:21.960
that we had given enough aid and support,&nbsp;
but essentially, he lost the civil war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:27.120
and we wanted to cut our losses there.&nbsp;
So the Truman administration essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:34.740
resigns itself to Mao at some point, taking over&nbsp;
Taiwan and absorbing that into the PRC. But then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.740 --> 00:12:39.840
the next major event that happens that changes&nbsp;
the course of history really is the Korean War,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:43.620
which erupts in the summer of 1950.
British Movietone: The peoples of the free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.620 --> 00:12:47.820
world, acting under the United Nations, have&nbsp;
given their answer, and the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.820 --> 00:12:53.460
in a swift, bold decision has already gone&nbsp;
into action. Air power first and sea power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:57.840
with the Seventh Fleet ordered to protect Formosa&nbsp;
together with reinforcements for the Philippines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.840 --> 00:13:02.820
and more aid to French Indo-China.
SACKS: President Truman sends the U.S. Navy into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.820 --> 00:13:10.380
the Taiwan Strait to prevent Mao from using this&nbsp;
as an opportunity to take over Taiwan. As the Cold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.380 --> 00:13:16.500
War had solidified, the United States didn't want&nbsp;
to see the communist North Korea attacking South&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.500 --> 00:13:23.580
Korea and Mao's communist forces attacking Taiwan&nbsp;
and wanted to lock in the separation, at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.580 --> 00:13:30.180
for the time being. And the Truman Administration&nbsp;
changes the U.S. position on Taiwan, that Taiwan's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:35.940
status remained undetermined and had to await the&nbsp;
restoration of peace in the Asia-Pacific. 
&nbsp;

00:13:35.940 --> 00:13:40.800
Harry S. Truman: When Japan returns to its place&nbsp;
in the family of nations and with the people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.800 --> 00:13:49.260
of Korea secure, free, and united, it should be&nbsp;
possible to find ways to settle other problems in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.260 --> 00:13:55.200
the Pacific which now threaten the peace.
LIN: Over time, people began to realize that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.200 --> 00:13:59.580
Beijing's government, the People's Republic of&nbsp;
China, was going to stay, and many countries began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.580 --> 00:14:04.320
to switch recognition. I would say the turning&nbsp;
point was in 1971, when the United Nations General&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:09.840
Assembly voted to replace the representative&nbsp;
of China with the People’s Republic of China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.840 --> 00:14:14.580
the government based in Beijing. And that was a&nbsp;
turning point where many people or many countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.580 --> 00:14:19.560
thought to themselves, "The United Nations has&nbsp;
replaced the official representation. We should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.560 --> 00:14:23.700
probably switch recognition as well." Under&nbsp;
the Nixon administration, Henry Kissinger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.700 --> 00:14:28.320
who was the national security advisor secretly&nbsp;
went to Beijing to negotiate with Zhou Enlai,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.320 --> 00:14:32.940
who was the foreign minister at the time, to&nbsp;
negotiate a rapprochement basically. So this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.940 --> 00:14:37.680
was during the Cold War, when the United States&nbsp;
was in this kind of high level conflict with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:42.420
Soviet Union. And Nixon and Kissinger came up with&nbsp;
a strategy to isolate the Soviet Union by picking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.420 --> 00:14:48.180
off one of their allies. And so in exchange for&nbsp;
the United States kind of re-engaging the PRC,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.180 --> 00:14:54.660
Zhou Enlai asked Kissinger to take a position&nbsp;
on Taiwan. And so this eventually came out to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.660 --> 00:14:59.280
be what's called the One China policy.
Richard Nixon: I want to put our policy in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.280 --> 00:15:04.080
the clearest possible context. Our&nbsp;
action in seeking a new relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:08.940
with the People’s Republic of China will&nbsp;
not be at the expense of our old friends.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.480 --> 00:15:14.760
I have taken this action because of my&nbsp;
profound conviction that all nations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.760 --> 00:15:20.640
will gain from a reduction of tensions and&nbsp;
a better relationship between the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:28.020
States and the People’s Republic of China.
SACKS: So, what that means is essentially the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.020 --> 00:15:34.020
United States recognizes the People's Republic&nbsp;
of China as the sole legal government of China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.020 --> 00:15:39.960
and it acknowledges the Chinese position that&nbsp;
Taiwan is a part of China. But importantly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.960 --> 00:15:45.660
it does not endorse or recognize China's view&nbsp;
that Taiwan is a part of China. So the One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.660 --> 00:15:52.140
China policy has a lot of ambiguity built into&nbsp;
it, and the U.S. relationship with Taiwan has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.140 --> 00:15:57.600
evolved over time. But the core tenets of the One&nbsp;
China policy, which is really about diplomatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.600 --> 00:16:02.040
relations with Taiwan, remain in place.
Jimmy Carter: The government of the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:06.646
States of America acknowledges the Chinese&nbsp;
position that there is but one China.
&nbsp;

00:16:06.646 --> 00:16:10.440
Bill Clinton: I believe that both&nbsp;
China and Taiwan want to stay on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.440 --> 00:16:14.100
the path to prosperity and dialogue.
George W. Bush: The issue that really is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.100 --> 00:16:18.540
issue of the future, in many ways, is China.
Barack Obama: There’s probably no bilateral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.540 --> 00:16:21.540
relationship that carries more significance.
Donald Trump: I don’t know why we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.540 --> 00:16:25.740
to be bound by a One China policy.
Joe Biden: We agree with a One China policy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.740 --> 00:16:30.300
but the idea that it can be taken by&nbsp;
force is just not appropriate.
&nbsp;

00:16:30.300 --> 00:16:35.700
SIERRA: The One China policy has remained&nbsp;
in place, and helped keep the peace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:40.860
under every president since Richard Nixon.
SIERRA: However the executive branch was not the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.860 --> 00:16:45.960
only part of government that was interested&nbsp;
in the China-Taiwan relationship. In 1979,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.960 --> 00:16:53.760
Congress passed the Taiwan Relations Act, or TRA,&nbsp;
which created the American Institute in Taiwan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.760 --> 00:17:00.180
a nonprofit organization that oversees America's&nbsp;
unofficial relationship with Taiwan. Many see it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.180 --> 00:17:04.740
as a kind of unofficial embassy.
SIERRA: The TRA holds that the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.740 --> 00:17:10.560
States would view "any effort to determine&nbsp;
the future of Taiwan by other than peaceful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.560 --> 00:17:17.040
means...a threat to the peace and security of&nbsp;
the Western Pacific area and of grave concern."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.040 --> 00:17:22.800
It also pledges that the United States will&nbsp;
provide Taiwan with defensive arms. While the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.800 --> 00:17:27.780
TRA does not obligate the United States to&nbsp;
defend Taiwan, it does state that the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.780 --> 00:17:32.940
States will maintain the capacity to do so. 
SIERRA: The right to defend Taiwan, but not the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.940 --> 00:17:39.900
obligation. A nonprofit organization but not an&nbsp;
embassy. It’s a careful dance, and as a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.900 --> 00:17:45.240
visits between American and Taiwanese leaders&nbsp;
are rare. When they do happen, they tend to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.240 --> 00:17:51.240
make headlines. You may recall that last year&nbsp;
former Speaker of the House, Nancy Pelosi, took a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.240 --> 00:17:57.000
trip to Taiwan - the first time a high level U.S.&nbsp;
official had traveled there in over 30 years.
&nbsp;

00:17:57.000 --> 00:18:01.920
SIERRA: China wasn’t too happy about it.
PBS NewsHour: As soon as Pelosi arrived,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.920 --> 00:18:06.406
China announced the beginning of air and sea&nbsp;
military exercises around the island.
&nbsp;

00:18:06.406 --> 00:18:11.520
PBS NewsHour: Ahead of the visit, China put its&nbsp;
military on high-alert, and flew fighter jets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.520 --> 00:18:13.540
close to Taiwan.
SIERRA:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.140 --> 00:18:20.340
I want to work through this situation and&nbsp;
look at it from every side. And I think it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.340 --> 00:18:26.460
only fair that we start with Taiwan itself,&nbsp;
the little guy caught between giants. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.460 --> 00:18:32.520
what do Taiwanese people want? 
LIN: So the National Chengchi University,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.520 --> 00:18:37.260
which is one of the top universities in Taiwan,&nbsp;
has an election study center that, since 1992, has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.260 --> 00:18:43.860
conducted biannual polls. One is a poll about what&nbsp;
should Taiwan do about its cross-strait policy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.860 --> 00:18:48.300
about its relationship with Beijing? And it asks&nbsp;
questions, should we just maintain the status quo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.300 --> 00:18:52.620
indefinitely, or should we maintain the status quo&nbsp;
and then decide at a later date to maybe change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.620 --> 00:18:57.900
it? Should we seek to unify with China, or should&nbsp;
we seek independence? And independence in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.900 --> 00:19:01.920
context means something more like changing the&nbsp;
constitution, so that we're no longer officially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.920 --> 00:19:05.820
the Republic of China, and maybe changing the name&nbsp;
in something like the Republic of Taiwan, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.820 --> 00:19:10.860
Beijing has said is a red line. The polls show&nbsp;
overwhelmingly that Taiwanese prefer the status&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.860 --> 00:19:16.500
quo. So if you add together all the status quo&nbsp;
answers it's around like 88 percent of Taiwanese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.500 --> 00:19:20.280
people want to maintain the status quo. And so&nbsp;
I would say that Taiwanese people recognize that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.280 --> 00:19:25.560
there is a significant threat to their existence,&nbsp;
and you see it play out in the polls. And there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.560 --> 00:19:29.460
have been other polls too that have asked kind&nbsp;
of hypothetical questions like, if Beijing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:29.460 --> 00:19:34.320
didn't threaten us, what would be your preferred&nbsp;
kind of outcome? And you can see that it changes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:34.320 --> 00:19:38.520
dramatically where many Taiwanese people actually&nbsp;
prefer some sort of independence or some sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.520 --> 00:19:42.840
greater assertion of its sovereignty. And so this&nbsp;
kind of tells us that there's an understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.840 --> 00:19:49.260
about - pragmatism about that threat. The other&nbsp;
poll is a question about Taiwanese identity. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:49.260 --> 00:19:54.060
in 1992, when the polls first began, there was a&nbsp;
large number of people who identified as Chinese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.060 --> 00:19:58.980
and only as Chinese, I think around 30 percent,&nbsp;
and the majority of people identified as both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.980 --> 00:20:04.980
Chinese and Taiwanese. Today, in 2023, 60 percent&nbsp;
of people in Taiwan identify as Taiwanese and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.980 --> 00:20:10.800
only as Taiwanese, whereas only about 3 percent&nbsp;
identify as Chinese and only as Chinese. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.800 --> 00:20:16.020
this is a dramatic shift in the past thirty years,&nbsp;
where Chinese identity has really dropped off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.020 --> 00:20:21.120
and Taiwanese identity - solely being&nbsp;
Taiwanese - has become the majority.
&nbsp;

00:20:21.120 --> 00:20:26.820
SIERRA: Given the danger and tensions of&nbsp;
the moment, it’s perhaps no surprise that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.820 --> 00:20:32.700
a majority of Taiwanese would prefer the status&nbsp;
quo to gambling their freedom in a conflict. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:32.700 --> 00:20:37.500
it’s also telling that when asked what they&nbsp;
would choose if there was no danger, most say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.500 --> 00:20:42.420
they would prefer some form of independence. 
SIERRA: Watching all of this is the United States.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:42.420 --> 00:20:49.740
Yes, Beijing will decide whether or not to invade&nbsp;
Taiwan. But the U.S. must decide whether or not to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:49.740 --> 00:20:52.240
defend Taiwan if it happens. 
SIERRA:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:54.840 --> 00:20:59.100
So, let's talk about the United States.&nbsp;
Over the last year, there's been more and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.100 --> 00:21:05.340
more news suggesting that the U.S. is&nbsp;
perhaps on a path to war over Taiwan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:05.340 --> 00:21:10.320
So I just want to get this straight. The U.S.&nbsp;
doesn't recognize Taiwan as a country. Taiwan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:10.320 --> 00:21:18.960
is very far from the U.S. mainland. So why on&nbsp;
Earth would the U.S. risk a war over Taiwan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:18.960 --> 00:21:24.840
with its most powerful adversary, China?
SACKS: Yeah, it's a very important question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:24.840 --> 00:21:29.700
and I think it's something that we should&nbsp;
discuss and have a robust debate about in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:29.700 --> 00:21:35.100
the United States. So I'll just outline what&nbsp;
I see as some of the U.S. interests at stake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:35.100 --> 00:21:40.920
in the Taiwan Strait. Number one, pull out a&nbsp;
map. Look at where Taiwan is located. It is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:40.920 --> 00:21:45.840
located at a critical point in the first island&nbsp;
chain, which stretches from Japan down through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.840 --> 00:21:51.720
Taiwan and into the Philippines. Japan and the&nbsp;
Philippines, of course, two very important U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:51.720 --> 00:21:57.420
allies. And with the integrity of the first&nbsp;
island chain the way it is right now, it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:57.420 --> 00:22:04.560
very difficult for China to project military power&nbsp;
far beyond its shores to threaten U.S. allies or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:04.560 --> 00:22:10.020
even the United States itself. But if it annexes&nbsp;
Taiwan and stations its military on the island,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:10.020 --> 00:22:17.400
then that all changes. China would be less than&nbsp;
one hundred miles away from Japan. It would be far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.400 --> 00:22:22.320
closer to the Philippines. It would make a U.S.&nbsp;
defense of those allies incredibly difficult.
&nbsp;

00:22:22.320 --> 00:22:27.660
SIERRA: For years now, China has been&nbsp;
expanding its military presence in the Pacific,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:27.660 --> 00:22:32.640
building installations in disputed areas,&nbsp;
and generally bullying smaller neighbors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:32.640 --> 00:22:38.520
over territory in the South China Sea. This&nbsp;
has led some strategists to fear that the U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:38.520 --> 00:22:43.440
could eventually lose its ability to project&nbsp;
power into the Pacific, something the country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:43.440 --> 00:22:49.320
has been able to do since World War II. 
SACKS: You have to reckon, I think, with what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:49.320 --> 00:22:55.620
happens in the strategic thinking and the calculus&nbsp;
of U.S. allies the day after a Chinese takeover of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:55.620 --> 00:23:02.160
Taiwan. In my view, Japanese leaders view this&nbsp;
as a critical interest for Japanese security,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:02.160 --> 00:23:09.960
and if China takes over Taiwan, Japan has grave&nbsp;
doubts as to whether the United States can or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:09.960 --> 00:23:16.560
would come to its defense. And a more powerful&nbsp;
country like Japan, it either seeks to accommodate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:16.560 --> 00:23:21.900
China's interests and moves closer to China,&nbsp;
but it could also pursue some form of strategic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:21.900 --> 00:23:27.840
autonomy, which would likely involve developing&nbsp;
nuclear weapons of its own and a major increase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:27.840 --> 00:23:33.660
in defense capabilities and spending. Relatively&nbsp;
weaker countries like the Philippines, to me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:33.660 --> 00:23:39.780
I just think that they essentially decide&nbsp;
to accommodate China's interests and live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:39.780 --> 00:23:46.140
with the fact that China is the new dominant&nbsp;
power in the Indo-Pacific, and the era of U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:46.140 --> 00:23:51.240
influence is kind of over. And so I think&nbsp;
this has major implications for U.S. allies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:51.240 --> 00:23:57.540
and partnerships in the region, which is our most&nbsp;
important asymmetrical advantage over China when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:57.540 --> 00:24:02.160
we're thinking of how we compete with China in&nbsp;
the long-term competition. I think you look at it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:02.160 --> 00:24:07.740
economically - what is our interest in deterring a&nbsp;
conflict in the Taiwan Strait? Over 90 percent of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:07.740 --> 00:24:14.340
the world's most advanced semiconductors are made&nbsp;
in Taiwan. Around 70 percent of all semiconductors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:14.340 --> 00:24:19.800
in the world are manufactured in Taiwan. So&nbsp;
regardless of whether the United States chooses to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:19.800 --> 00:24:26.940
intervene on Taiwan's behalf, if deterrence fails&nbsp;
and China uses force, we're going to see a global&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:26.940 --> 00:24:32.880
economic depression with trillions of dollars&nbsp;
shaved off global GDP that will be incredibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:32.880 --> 00:24:38.040
difficult to recover from, given the nature&nbsp;
of the semiconductor industry, how difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:38.040 --> 00:24:45.120
it is to build those facilities elsewhere and&nbsp;
get up to speed. So that really emphasizes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:45.120 --> 00:24:51.060
importance of deterrence, in my view.
SIERRA: We actually did a whole episode on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:51.060 --> 00:24:55.860
semiconductors and how important they are&nbsp;
to the U.S. In it, we take a really deep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:55.860 --> 00:25:00.360
dive into how the whole world relies&nbsp;
on Taiwan every day to keep the tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:00.360 --> 00:25:05.220
economy running. You should check it out.
SIERRA: Bottom line, all of these factors together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:05.220 --> 00:25:10.860
make it a very real possibility that the U.S.&nbsp;
could choose to intervene if China invades.
&nbsp;

00:25:10.860 --> 00:25:18.660
SIERRA: So a bit of a logistics question. Let's&nbsp;
say that the U.S. commits to defending Taiwan from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:18.660 --> 00:25:23.880
an invasion. How would we even do that? Because&nbsp;
China is what, a hundred miles from Taiwan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:23.880 --> 00:25:29.880
whereas we are thousands of miles away.
SACKS: Yeah. So geography I think does cut in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:29.880 --> 00:25:34.140
China's favor, but we shouldn't oversimplify&nbsp;
this and say, "Well, this would just be a walk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:34.140 --> 00:25:39.960
in the park for the People's Liberation Army."&nbsp;
We're still talking about a need to cross ninety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:39.960 --> 00:25:47.460
miles of treacherous waters, where they would&nbsp;
be targeted by Taiwanese missiles and ships&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:47.460 --> 00:25:54.060
and potentially U.S. submarines, again, missiles&nbsp;
and ships as well. And so it would be incredibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:54.060 --> 00:26:01.440
difficult for China to pull that off, and China&nbsp;
has to also anticipate that even if it lands on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:01.440 --> 00:26:07.920
Taiwan and successfully builds a beachhead that&nbsp;
it would face urban warfare and significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:07.920 --> 00:26:13.140
resistance from the Taiwanese people. So I don't&nbsp;
think that the Chinese leadership underestimates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:13.140 --> 00:26:18.180
the difficulty of pulling this off. Now,&nbsp;
from the U.S. perspective, I think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:18.180 --> 00:26:23.700
a critical thing that we have to think about is&nbsp;
Japan's role in a conflict. Yes, we're far away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:23.700 --> 00:26:30.060
but the United States does have over fifty&nbsp;
thousand troops in Japan, as well as advanced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:30.060 --> 00:26:37.020
capabilities that would be relevant for a conflict&nbsp;
over Taiwan. And so this is a big question mark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:37.020 --> 00:26:43.200
though. To what extent is Japan involved in a&nbsp;
Taiwan contingency? To what extent does it support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:43.200 --> 00:26:49.740
U.S. operations, assist in U.S. operations?&nbsp;
But if there is full Japanese support and if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:49.740 --> 00:26:56.040
we do more to build our ability to work with our&nbsp;
Japanese allies and to really operate together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:56.040 --> 00:27:01.380
then we still have enduring strengths over the&nbsp;
PLA that we can bring to bear in the Taiwan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:01.380 --> 00:27:08.340
Strait. And we should also note that, yes, China&nbsp;
has overseen a remarkable modernization of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:08.340 --> 00:27:15.360
People's Liberation Army, and on paper, the PLA is&nbsp;
very strong, but these officers, most of them have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:15.360 --> 00:27:21.600
never seen combat. The last time China fought a&nbsp;
war was in 1979 with Vietnam, and the last time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:21.600 --> 00:27:29.220
that it fought a really large-scale war was the&nbsp;
Korean War in 1950. So the United States, yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:29.220 --> 00:27:34.860
we have big gaps, and we need to address those.&nbsp;
The war in Ukraine has shown that our defense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:34.860 --> 00:27:39.300
industrial base is not where it needs to be, for&nbsp;
instance, and we need to take steps to address&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:39.300 --> 00:27:45.720
those shortfalls. But I don't think that we should&nbsp;
assess this - the ‘if China decides to go for it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:45.720 --> 00:27:50.400
it can achieve a fait accompli,’ and basically,&nbsp;
we should just throw up our hands and say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:50.400 --> 00:27:53.880
"You've succeeded."
SIERRA: What would a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:53.880 --> 00:27:59.640
conflict over Taiwan do to the world?
LIN: It depends on the kind of conflict. Kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:59.640 --> 00:28:05.280
of a worst case scenario is a full on amphibious&nbsp;
invasion and occupation of Taiwan by the PLA,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:05.280 --> 00:28:09.000
the Chinese military. And that would be, in my&nbsp;
opinion, would be completely disastrous. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:09.000 --> 00:28:13.980
it would draw in other powers into a conflict. I&nbsp;
think the United States, even if it doesn't really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:13.980 --> 00:28:19.260
respond by deploying the military to defend&nbsp;
Taiwan, I think that it would involve lots of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:19.260 --> 00:28:23.340
punitive measures, economic punitive measures&nbsp;
that we're seeing towards Russia currently.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:23.340 --> 00:28:28.680
But it would be much more drastic because Russia&nbsp;
is an important energy supplier, but its economy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:28.680 --> 00:28:33.120
is not as integrated into the global economy,&nbsp;
whereas China is the so-called ‘factory of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:33.120 --> 00:28:39.360
world.’ And any kind of military action would&nbsp;
take offline, not just Taiwan's manufacturing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:39.360 --> 00:28:44.400
but also I think would have an effect on China's&nbsp;
inclusion in the global economy. And that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:44.400 --> 00:28:51.900
would just grind things to a total halt. 
SIERRA: What is the risk of escalation here? What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:51.900 --> 00:28:56.760
about the possibility of nuclear escalation?
SACKS: So I think that's a really important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:56.760 --> 00:29:01.680
question, and I think frankly, that's one&nbsp;
that we haven't really contended with enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:01.680 --> 00:29:06.360
because it's so difficult to wrap our&nbsp;
heads around it. What we do know, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:06.360 --> 00:29:13.560
is that China is making remarkable advances in&nbsp;
its nuclear arsenal, that it is now in the midst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:13.560 --> 00:29:20.580
of a huge buildup in its nuclear arsenal. And,&nbsp;
in my view, it has direct relevance to Taiwan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:20.580 --> 00:29:27.480
So I don't have a good answer. Nobody knows&nbsp;
whether a conflict over Taiwan could be kept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:27.480 --> 00:29:32.760
to conventional weaponry, but I also think&nbsp;
nobody knows for sure that a conflict over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:32.760 --> 00:29:39.060
Taiwan would immediately go nuclear. Again,&nbsp;
we can make well-educated guesses about how a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:39.060 --> 00:29:43.800
conflict would play out, but I think anybody who&nbsp;
tells you that they know for certain one way or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:43.800 --> 00:29:49.920
the other is not being truthful.
SIERRA: So it seems pretty clear that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:49.920 --> 00:29:54.480
this is something every person on Earth&nbsp;
really has an interest in preventing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:54.480 --> 00:30:01.440
So what can be done to avoid the conflict?
SACKS: At a fundamental level, I think, that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:01.440 --> 00:30:06.960
political foundation or framework should remain in&nbsp;
place, which is that the United States maintains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:06.960 --> 00:30:14.040
its One China policy, does not recognize Taiwan&nbsp;
as an independent country. Taiwan acts responsibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:14.040 --> 00:30:19.260
and doesn't seek to declare independence&nbsp;
for itself. And within that context, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:19.260 --> 00:30:25.380
I think we need to do a lot more to bolster&nbsp;
deterrence in the Taiwan Strait. That's things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:25.380 --> 00:30:31.860
like for the United States to prioritize things&nbsp;
like stealth bombers, submarines, long range&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:31.860 --> 00:30:39.060
missiles that would all be incredibly important in&nbsp;
a conflict over Taiwan, to assist Taiwan in also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:39.060 --> 00:30:45.600
overhauling its military, doing a lot more to help&nbsp;
train Taiwan's military to prepare for a conflict,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:45.600 --> 00:30:51.360
and also thinking through things with Taiwan&nbsp;
in terms of civil resilience and its ability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:51.360 --> 00:30:57.660
to hold out during a conflict. Taiwan imports&nbsp;
nearly all of its energy. It is a net importer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:57.660 --> 00:31:04.560
of food and medical supplies. So, how can we&nbsp;
help Taiwan shore up those vulnerabilities to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:04.560 --> 00:31:11.280
ensure that it can hold out long enough to enable&nbsp;
the United States to assist Taiwan in the face of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:11.280 --> 00:31:19.080
Chinese aggression? I do think that we want to do&nbsp;
what we can to minimize the urgency that the PRC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:19.080 --> 00:31:25.440
feels with respect to Taiwan. So I don't think we&nbsp;
want to feed fears that this is slipping away and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:25.440 --> 00:31:32.100
that time is not on its side. My own view is that&nbsp;
there's only so much we can do in that regard, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:32.100 --> 00:31:37.440
we shouldn't play into those fears. And I think&nbsp;
that with Taiwan, there's a whole bucket of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:37.440 --> 00:31:41.760
we do with Taiwan, and we need to do a lot more&nbsp;
to ensure its economic competitiveness as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:41.760 --> 00:31:46.680
I think we should negotiate a bilateral trade&nbsp;
agreement with Taiwan. We should work with Taiwan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:46.680 --> 00:31:52.800
on global supply chain issues. We should help&nbsp;
ensure that the PRC is not able to economically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:52.800 --> 00:31:58.320
marginalize Taiwan. But there's a whole bucket,&nbsp;
also, of things that we don't do with Taiwan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:58.320 --> 00:32:05.280
that, in this scenario, are just as important. So&nbsp;
in my view, we should do less that is heavy on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:05.280 --> 00:32:12.180
symbolism side, which does play into this Chinese&nbsp;
fear and is likely to provoke a PRC response&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:12.180 --> 00:32:18.360
without actually meaningfully improving Taiwan's&nbsp;
defensive capabilities or its resilience, but we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:18.360 --> 00:32:24.300
should do a lot more on the substantive side.
SIERRA: You know, in certain circles, it can feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:24.300 --> 00:32:28.620
like people think conflict between the&nbsp;
United States and China is inevitable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:28.620 --> 00:32:34.260
and that this could be sort of a self-fulfilling&nbsp;
prophecy, the logic that sooner or later China&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:34.260 --> 00:32:40.800
will move to take Taiwan back, the U.S. will be&nbsp;
compelled to intervene, and obviously we all, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:40.800 --> 00:32:46.680
Taiwanese people especially, have an interest in&nbsp;
preventing this. So how do you sort of avoid that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:46.680 --> 00:32:51.600
mindset? How do we remain open to off-ramps?
LIN: Yeah, I think that's really important because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:51.600 --> 00:32:55.920
I think there are elements of, especially&nbsp;
at the U.S. foreign policy circles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:55.920 --> 00:32:59.940
that there are some who think that conflict is&nbsp;
inevitable. And I don't think that's the case. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:59.940 --> 00:33:04.980
think that we have to keep in mind that war would&nbsp;
be tremendously costly for all parties involved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:04.980 --> 00:33:09.300
and we have to assume that there's still some&nbsp;
rationality involved in foreign policy making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:09.300 --> 00:33:14.340
for Beijing, that they're not going to&nbsp;
act as recklessly as some might assume,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:14.340 --> 00:33:20.220
that even though there is maybe a window of&nbsp;
military advantage, that doesn't necessarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:20.220 --> 00:33:26.220
mean that they will make a political decision&nbsp;
to use the military for a political action. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:26.220 --> 00:33:31.680
I would just make sure that everybody is reminded&nbsp;
of that part. There are lots of risks involved and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:31.680 --> 00:33:36.600
there are lots of costs, and I think that there&nbsp;
are ways that we should keep off-ramps open,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:36.600 --> 00:33:41.700
as you've said, don't make any reckless&nbsp;
unilateral actions, and then hope that all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:41.700 --> 00:33:46.020
parties involved also see that the best&nbsp;
way to pursue a future is to maintain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:46.020 --> 00:33:51.240
the status quo and not rock the boat. 
SACKS: Our aim shouldn't be to solve the Taiwan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:51.240 --> 00:33:56.760
issue, but instead to continue to finesse this in&nbsp;
such a way where China doesn't feel compelled to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:56.760 --> 00:34:02.700
attack Taiwan, and Taiwan can feel confident&nbsp;
in its ability to maintain its democratic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:02.700 --> 00:34:09.000
institutions and its economic vitality. The&nbsp;
overriding goal of U.S. policy, in my view,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:09.000 --> 00:34:15.420
is to ensure that this remains difficult enough&nbsp;
for Chinese leadership that when Xi Jinping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:15.420 --> 00:34:19.740
wakes up in the morning and he looks across the&nbsp;
Taiwan Strait, he says, today is not the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:20.280 --> 00:34:26.280
that he has real doubts in his mind as to whether&nbsp;
the Chinese military can accomplish the objective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:26.280 --> 00:34:28.000
he sets out.
